metadata.rules_text:
{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent upward bias in realized prices vs model from ~day 10 onward: model underestimates sustained rallies (true path trends higher and stays elevated while model stays near 68–70). Apply a positive drift correction that increases with horizon (small at 1–5d, larger at 10–30d).",
    "Trend/lag error: model reacts too slowly to regime shifts (fails to follow the step-up from ~69 to 72–73). Add a trend-following component: if last 5–10d slope is positive, lift the whole forward curve, especially mid/long horizons.",
    "Spike underestimation: on strong up-move days the model predicts only modest bumps (e.g., around the 72–73 area). Use a convexity adjustment: when recent volatility or 1–3d returns are high and positive, increase forecast amplitude (don’t cap near recent mean).",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration at long horizon: model often assumes reversion back toward ~69–70 while outcomes remain elevated (or only revert late). Reduce mean-reversion strength beyond ~10d during uptrends; delay reversion timing.",
    "Horizon-specific: 1–5d forecasts are relatively close (small errors), but 10–25d errors are systematically negative (under-forecast). Apply piecewise bias: near-term ~0, mid-term +1 to +2 EUR, long-term +2 to +4 EUR (scaled by recent trend strength).",
    "Occasional late-horizon overshoot when model finally turns bullish (e.g., projecting 72–74) but realized prices revert (Example 5 tail). Add a terminal damping rule: if forecast implies acceleration while recent realized momentum is flattening, cap the last 5–10d forecasts toward the prevailing level."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially during rising regimes. Apply an upward level shift (≈ +1 to +3 EUR) as baseline, larger when trend is up.",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: after a downside inflection in history, the model projects sideways-to-down while the market rebounds strongly. Add a positive trend-following correction when the last 5–10 days show stabilization after a dip.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors grow with horizon; days ~10–25 are most underpredicted during rallies. Use a ramping correction that increases with lead time (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Missed rally amplitude: during sustained upswings, the model caps prices around ~69–71 while outcomes reach ~72–73. Introduce a volatility/convexity uplift when recent realized volatility is elevated and momentum is positive.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot risk after sharp peaks: when the model finally turns bullish late in the window, it can overshoot if the market mean-reverts (seen in the case where realized drops while forecast stays high). Add a mean-reversion dampener beyond ~day 20 when the forecast implies a continued steep rise but recent spot has started to roll over."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show an upswing, the model underpredicts the level over the next ~10–20 days. Apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small at days 1–3, larger by days 10–20).",
    "Trend/lag error: the forecast path reacts too slowly to strong upward moves (it stays near ~68–70 while realized moves into ~71–73). Add a trend-following term proportional to recent momentum (e.g., last 5–7 day slope) for horizons ~5–20 days.",
    "Peak underestimation: during rally phases the model misses the amplitude of local highs (notably around day ~8–22 in the examples). Inflate predicted peaks by adding a convex adjustment (bigger add-on when forecast is already above its recent mean).",
    "Late-horizon overshoot after regime change: beyond ~20–30 days the model can remain too high when the realized series starts reverting/lowering (e.g., realized drops to ~68–67 while forecast stays ~70–73). Introduce mean-reversion pull toward the last observed spot for horizons >~20 days.",
    "Horizon-dependent calibration: use a piecewise correction—(i) days 1–5: minimal adjustment; (ii) days 6–20: upward bias/trend correction; (iii) days 21–30: damp forecasts toward spot/long-run mean to avoid overextension."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Uptrend underestimation: When the last 5–10 days show a clear upward drift, the model is systematically too low versus realized levels. Apply a positive level shift of ~+1.5 to +3.0 EUR early in the horizon (days 1–20), tapering toward ~0 by days 25–30.",
    "Turning-point lag: The forecast path tends to react with delay to regime changes (especially transitions into rallies). If momentum (e.g., 5d return) turns positive after a flat/down period, steepen the first half of the forecast (increase day-to-day slope) rather than just shifting the level.",
    "Peak/late-horizon overstay: After strong rallies, the model often keeps prices elevated too long and misses subsequent sell-offs into the far horizon. Add stronger mean-reversion in days 20–30 (pull forecasts toward the 30d history mean/last price), especially if recent volatility/spike is high.",
    "Spike handling asymmetry: The model underpredicts upside spikes (true outcomes jump higher than forecast) but can overpredict persistence afterward. Heuristic: allow higher short-term amplitude (raise near-term highs) but enforce faster decay back toward trend/mean beyond ~2 weeks.",
    "Horizon-specific calibration: Net bias is positive in the near term during rallies (needs upward correction), but becomes neutral-to-negative in the far tail after big moves (needs downward/mean-reverting correction). Implement a time-varying bias: +b for days 1–15, 0 for days 16–20, −b/2 (or mean-reversion pull) for days 21–30 when prior 10d trend is strongly positive."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent underprediction in rising regimes: when the last ~5–10 days show a clear uptrend, add an upward bias (model is typically ~+1 to +3 EUR too low across the first ~15–20 forecast days).",
    "Trend/level lag: the forecast path tends to follow the prior level and reacts too slowly to breakouts; apply a faster trend component (increase slope) in the first half of the horizon when momentum is positive.",
    "Peak underestimation: local highs are systematically capped too low (true series makes higher highs than forecast); widen upside range or add extra uplift near projected peaks during strong rallies.",
    "Late-horizon reversal error: beyond ~day 20 the model often stays too high and misses sharp sell-offs (fails to mean-revert downward); introduce stronger mean reversion / downside adjustment in the back half of the horizon, especially after extended rallies.",
    "Oversmoothing around regime shifts: after a sudden drop, the model remains elevated for too long; if recent realized volatility increases or a large down day occurs, pull the entire forecast curve down more aggressively for days ~10–30."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show a clear uptrend, add an upward level shift to the whole path (model underestimates by ~+1 to +3 €/t, often larger around days 7–16).",
    "Turning-point lag: after a local peak/trough in the last ~3–7 days, the forecast tends to continue the prior direction too long; damp the first 1–2 weeks’ trend component and pull forecasts toward the recent 10–20d mean.",
    "Overshoot on late-horizon strength: the model often keeps prices elevated into weeks 3–4 when the realized path mean-reverts/downshifts; apply stronger mean reversion beyond ~day 15 (reduce levels by ~1–4 €/t when the model stays > recent mean).",
    "Underestimation of short-term volatility/spike amplitude: in the first ~10 days after an upswing, realized highs exceed forecast highs; widen bands and increase peak response (don’t cap rallies too early).",
    "Regime-change miss (sharp selloff): when recent history includes a sudden drop (large negative daily moves), the model is too high in the last 5–10 forecast days; apply a negative adjustment that grows with horizon (increasing downshift into days 20–30)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Regime-shift/mean-reversion miss: when the recent 30d history contains a sharp upswing into the low-70s, the model extrapolates the rally and stays too high; true prices tend to mean-revert and roll over within ~2–3 weeks. Apply a downward adjustment starting around forecast day 10–15, increasing with horizon.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (first ~1–7 days) forecasts are slightly low during rising phases, but medium/long horizon (≈10–30 days) becomes systematically high (often by ~2–8 EUR) as the model fails to capture subsequent drawdowns. Use a bias curve that transitions from ~0 to negative after day ~7–10.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: after a local peak/spike (e.g., >+2 EUR move over a few days), the model predicts continued strength; realized path often fades. Cap post-spike upside by damping positive drift (e.g., shrink forecast changes toward 0 by ~30–50% for the next 5–10 days).",
    "Turning-point lag: the model reacts late to reversals—keeps forecasts elevated even as the true series starts declining. Add a reversal detector (e.g., consecutive lower closes or loss of momentum) that triggers faster pullback in the forecast level.",
    "Late-horizon tail risk: the model underestimates downside magnitude in sell-offs (true ends notably below forecast). Impose stronger mean-reversion/negative skew beyond ~day 20 when recent volatility is high or when prices are above the 30d average."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime bias: model underpredicts during sustained uptrends/rallies (≈ +1 to +3 EUR vs realized for ~days 1–15 in Examples 1–3). Apply a positive level shift when last 5–10 days show higher highs/higher lows.",
    "Turning-point/mean-reversion error: model is too slow to flip from bullish to bearish; after the peak it stays elevated and misses the selloff timing (Examples 2–5). Introduce earlier mean-reversion when momentum weakens (e.g., if 3–5 day return turns negative after a run-up).",
    "Crash magnitude underestimation: in sharp drawdowns the model remains far too high, especially beyond ~day 20 (Example 4: forecasts ~73–74 while realized falls to ~63–66). Add a downside stress adjustment when volatility expands and recent returns are negative.",
    "Horizon shape issue: forecast paths are too smooth/flat and do not capture the increasing probability of large moves with horizon; errors grow with lead time (notably days 20–30). Increase forecast variance and allow stronger drift changes at longer horizons.",
    "Spike handling: after upside spikes, the model tends to project continued strength rather than the observed fade; cap post-spike continuation and bias toward partial retracement over the next 5–15 days."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent high bias: the model level is systematically above realized prices by ~1–4 €/t on average, with the bias widening materially in the back half of the 30‑day horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: when the market transitions from rising to falling (or vice versa), the forecast continues the prior regime for too long; apply earlier inflection/mean-reversion than the model implies.",
    "Downside underreaction: in selloffs the model does not drop fast enough and keeps prices clustered near ~69–72 while outcomes slide into mid‑60s; increase negative drift when recent returns turn negative.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following short-term highs (e.g., ~71–73), the model projects persistence/continued strength, but realized prices mean-revert sharply; cap post-spike forecasts and force reversion toward the recent 30‑day average.",
    "Horizon-dependent correction: small adjustment needed in days 1–10 (often slightly low during immediate continuation), but from ~day 15 onward apply a stronger downward correction and steeper decay toward a lower terminal level.",
    "Variance compression: forecasts are too smooth (range too narrow) relative to realized moves; allow larger downside tails, especially after volatility increases."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime/turning-point bias: when the last ~5–10 days show a downside break (lower highs/lows), the model stays anchored to the prior level and overpredicts; apply a downward level shift early in the forecast (strongest from ~day 10 onward).",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: errors are small/slightly low in the first week during up-moves, but flip to persistent overprediction beyond ~2 weeks; introduce a negative slope correction that increases with horizon (e.g., subtract an increasing amount from day 8→30).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following a recent price spike to ~70–73, the model projects continued strength/plateau, while realized prices mean-revert sharply; cap post-spike forecasts and force faster mean reversion (reduce forecasts more aggressively after day ~7).",
    "Volatility compression: the model underestimates drawdown magnitude during sell-offs (true outcomes fall much more); widen downside tails by scaling negative moves (apply a multiplicative factor to forecasted declines or add extra downside shock when momentum turns negative).",
    "Lag in trend reversal: the model reacts ~1–2 weeks late to the transition from rally to sell-off; use a momentum filter (e.g., if 5d return < 0 and 10d MA rolling over) to switch to a bearish correction immediately rather than gradually."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime-change bias: when the last 5–10 days show a clear downside break (lower lows / accelerating drop), the model stays anchored to ~70–73 and overpredicts. Apply a bearish level-shift: subtract ~2–4 EUR for days 1–10 and ~5–9 EUR for days 11–30 in such setups.",
    "Horizon drift: forecast error grows with horizon (small near-term, large long-term). Use a horizon-dependent dampener that pulls the path toward the recent trend/spot: increase mean-reversion strength with lead time (e.g., 1x for days 1–5, 1.5x for days 6–15, 2x for days 16–30).",
    "Failure to capture post-peak selloffs: after a local peak/spike in history (e.g., ~71) the true series often mean-reverts sharply lower within 2–3 weeks, but the model projects persistence/uptrend. Add an “overshoot correction”: if recent max is within last 7 days and momentum turns negative, cap any forecast increases and impose a declining slope over days 10–30.",
    "Lag in trend reversal: the model reacts too slowly to turning points (keeps rising/flat while true turns down). Add a reversal trigger: if 3-day return < 0 and 10-day return < 0, force the next 10–20 forecast points to follow the recent negative slope (not flat).",
    "Over-smoothness / under-volatility: true outcomes show larger drawdowns than forecast. Inflate downside volatility in bearish regimes (widen lower tail): allow larger negative daily steps and avoid tight clustering around the mean."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts sit ~+3 to +8 EUR above realized levels, especially after regime shifts; apply a downward level correction (larger when recent trend turns down).",
    "Missed mean reversion after local highs: when history shows a peak/spike (e.g., ~72–73) followed by weakening, the model keeps projecting elevated prices; enforce stronger mean reversion toward the 10–20d average.",
    "Downtrend lag: once the market starts a sustained sell-off, the model reacts too slowly and predicts sideways/up; add a negative momentum term when last 5–10d return is negative and accelerating.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: overprediction grows with horizon (outer 2–4 weeks worst); increase the downward adjustment with forecast step (e.g., small cut days 1–5, larger cut days 10–30).",
    "Overshoot persistence: model assumes high-price persistence (sticky levels) while realized path breaks lower quickly; cap forecast persistence by limiting day-to-day increases when recent volatility is high and direction is down."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong upward bias beyond ~7–10 day horizon: forecasts stay in the 71–74 range while realized prices mean-revert and then trend sharply lower. Apply a negative level shift that increases with horizon (small in week 1, large by weeks 3–4).",
    "Regime-change blindness: when the last 30d history contains a rollover from ~72–73 into a downtrend, the model extrapolates the prior high regime instead of switching. Add a downtrend detector (e.g., negative 5–10d momentum / lower highs) that forces the forecast path to follow the new bearish regime.",
    "Overshoot and persistence after spikes: after local highs (~72–73), the model predicts continued strength for weeks, but realized prices reverse quickly and continue falling. Introduce spike-reversal logic: after a spike, cap upside follow-through and increase mean-reversion speed.",
    "Underestimates downside tail risk: realized outcomes show a large drawdown into low/mid-60s within 2–4 weeks, while forecasts never approach that range. Add a volatility/convexity adjustment: widen forecast bands and shift the mean downward when recent volatility rises or momentum turns negative.",
    "Horizon-specific drift error: day 1–5 errors are modest, but error accumulates with time (forecast path too flat/high). Apply a horizon-dependent drift correction (progressively more negative slope) rather than a constant offset."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Strong positive bias: model forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~day 10. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Missed regime shift / mean reversion: after the market rolls over, the model keeps projecting stable-to-rising prices. Add a mean-reversion correction that pulls forecasts toward the recent downtrend (e.g., last 10–15 day slope) rather than the prior range.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: early horizon (days 1–7) errors are small; mid/long horizon (days 10–30) errors become large and negative (forecast minus actual). Use a ramping correction (small at t<=7, larger by t≈30).",
    "Overshoot after local highs: when history contains a recent spike/high (e.g., ~72–73), the model extrapolates it upward, but outcomes revert sharply lower. Cap upside extrapolation and enforce reversion after highs (reduce forecast if last price is near a 30d high).",
    "Trend-lag: the model reacts too slowly to emerging declines; incorporate a momentum filter so that if recent returns are negative for several days, subtract an additional drift term from the forecast trajectory."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~day 10. Apply a downward level shift (increasing with horizon).",
    "Trend/turning-point miss: when the market transitions into a drawdown, the model keeps projecting flat-to-higher prices (fails to capture the downtrend). Add a regime filter: if recent returns are negative and volatility rising, tilt the entire path downward.",
    "Overshoot after local highs: following a short-term spike/new high in history, the model extrapolates strength, but outcomes mean-revert lower. Use mean-reversion: cap upside continuation and pull forecasts back toward a rolling mean after spikes.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small; mid/long horizon (days 10–30) errors become large and one-sided. Increase correction magnitude with lead time (e.g., linear ramp of downward adjustment).",
    "Volatility miscalibration: forecast paths are too smooth and stay elevated, while realizations show sharper sell-offs. Introduce asymmetric downside risk: widen lower bands / skew distribution downward when recent momentum is negative."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–12 €/t), especially beyond ~day 10. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Missed regime shift / mean reversion: when recent history shows a peak/spike (e.g., ~72–73) followed by weakening, the model extrapolates elevated levels instead of reverting. Add a mean-reversion pull toward the recent 30d average (or a lower post-break mean) starting around days 5–10.",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: short-horizon (days 1–5) errors are small, but mid/long horizon (days 10–30) errors grow sharply as the model stays near 72–75 while reality trends down to mid-60s. Use a horizon-scaled damping factor that compresses deviations from the anchor mean as horizon increases.",
    "Trend sign error after turning points: after a local top in history, the model continues forecasting flat-to-up; realized path trends down. If the last 5–10 days show decelerating gains or a rollover, force forecast drift negative for the next 2–3 weeks.",
    "Overreaction to recent highs: forecasts cluster tightly around recent highs (low dispersion) while outcomes show sustained selloff. Introduce an asymmetric correction: after upward spikes, cap upside continuation and increase probability/size of downward moves (stronger than after downward spikes)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias beyond ~1 week: the model systematically underpredicts medium-term rebounds/rallies. Apply an upward level adjustment that grows with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger from ~day 10 onward).",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: after a local bottom, the model turns up too slowly and misses the speed of recovery. If the last 5–10 days show a clear downswing into a trough, steepen the subsequent 1–3 week forecast slope upward.",
    "Underestimation of upside volatility: the model captures early path but misses later upside extensions (notably days ~20–30). Add a positive convexity term (increasing uplift) when recent realized volatility is elevated and momentum flips positive.",
    "Mean-reversion too strong / ceiling too low: forecasts revert toward low/mid-60s even when outcomes trend to upper-60s. Raise the implied long-run mean when the last observed price is below the 20–30d average but outcomes historically rebound.",
    "Horizon-specific correction: days 1–5 errors are small; the main miss is accumulation over days 10–30. Concentrate corrections on the back half of the forecast window rather than uniformly shifting the whole path."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift (≈ +0.5 to +1.5 €/t early horizon; rising to ≈ +2 to +5 €/t by days 20–30).",
    "Underreaction to uptrends/rallies: when the last 5–10 days show a rebound (e.g., from low 60s to mid/high 60s), the forecast path remains too flat/low. Add a trend-following adjustment proportional to recent slope (e.g., add 30–50% of the last-7-day linear trend to the next 2–3 weeks).",
    "Horizon-dependent drift error: forecast error increases with horizon (small at days 1–5, larger at days 15–30). Use a linearly increasing correction with lead time rather than a constant offset.",
    "Missed late-horizon spikes: the model fails to capture sharp upside moves near the end of the window (days ~23–30). Add a volatility/risk-premium uplift when recent realized volatility is elevated (e.g., if 10-day stdev rises, increase days 20–30 by an additional +1 to +3 €/t).",
    "Mean-reversion too strong after dips: following drawdowns in history, the model reverts to low/mid 60s while outcomes rebound to mid/high 60s. Reduce mean-reversion strength when the most recent price is near the 30-day low (i.e., allow higher rebound amplitude)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Late-horizon upside bias: from ~day 18–30 the model persistently underestimates rising markets; apply an increasing positive adjustment with horizon (≈ +1 to +2 €/t by day 20, rising to ≈ +3 to +6 €/t by day 30 when the realized path trends up).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the model reacts too slowly to regime shifts from downtrend to uptrend (misses the convexity of the rebound). If the last 5–10 days show a clear reversal (higher highs/higher lows), tilt the whole forecast path upward and steepen the slope.",
    "Underestimation after breakouts: when spot breaks above the recent 10–15d range, the model mean-reverts too aggressively and caps the move. In breakout conditions, reduce mean-reversion strength and allow higher terminal levels (raise upper tail).",
    "Horizon-dependent error structure: near-term (days 1–7) errors are small and roughly unbiased; keep minimal correction there, concentrate corrections on mid/long horizon (days 10–30).",
    "Asymmetry in rising vs falling regimes: the model performs worse in rallies than in declines (systematic negative bias during rallies). Use a regime switch: if recent momentum is positive, add a bullish bias; if momentum is negative/flat, apply little or no bias correction."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 10–30).",
    "Trend/acceleration underreaction: when the market transitions from a dip to a rally, the model lags and fails to capture the upswing magnitude. Add a momentum term based on the last 5–10 days’ slope/return to steepen the forecast path.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: forecast errors widen with time; treat long-horizon outputs as too mean-reverting. Reduce mean-reversion strength and allow a higher terminal level when recent trend is positive.",
    "Upside tail miss: the model underestimates peak/rally days (late-horizon highs). Introduce an asymmetric correction: larger upward adjustment when recent volatility is rising and recent returns are positive.",
    "Post-dip rebound mispricing: after a sharp drawdown in the history window, the model expects only a mild recovery; realized outcomes show stronger rebound. If last-30d includes a notable trough and subsequent bounce, boost days 5–20 forecasts."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Uptrend underestimation: when the last 5–10 days show a clear rebound/positive slope (e.g., >~+0.4 €/t per day over 5 days), the model systematically under-forecasts levels from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors are modest in the first ~7–10 days, then become increasingly positive (true > forecast) through days ~15–30. Use a ramp correction (e.g., add ~0.5–1 €/t around day 10 rising to ~3–5 €/t by day 30 in strong uptrends).",
    "Lag in regime change: after a local bottom followed by a sharp reversal, forecasts behave like a lagged continuation of the prior weakness. Detect ‘bottom + breakout’ (price crosses above recent 10-day average after making a 10–20 day low) and accelerate the forecast path upward (steepen slope) rather than keeping it flat/mean-reverting.",
    "Mean-reversion too strong: the model pulls prices back toward the recent mean, but realized outcomes trend higher and stay elevated. Reduce mean-reversion strength when realized volatility is rising and the last 3–5 closes are above the 10-day mean.",
    "Late-horizon ceiling/flattening: forecasts tend to cap/flatten in the mid-60s while true outcomes reach upper-60s/near-70. If history shows a rebound into the mid-60s, allow higher terminal levels (raise the long-run anchor/terminal value).",
    "Spike-follow-through underpricing: after an upside impulse (several consecutive up days), the model expects partial giveback; realized prices show follow-through. Add a momentum carry component for days 5–20 after an upside impulse instead of fading it."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Uptrend underestimation: when the last 5–10 days show a clear rebound/acceleration (e.g., +2 to +4 EUR from a local low), the model systematically under-forecasts the next 2–4 weeks. Apply a positive drift uplift that grows with horizon (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors are modest in the first ~7–10 days, but from ~day 12 onward the model is consistently too low versus realized prices. Use an increasing additive correction with lead time (e.g., +0.5 to +1 EUR in days 1–10, +2 to +4 EUR in days 11–20, +4 to +7 EUR in days 21–30, scaled to recent momentum).",
    "Lag/slow reaction to regime shift: after a drawdown followed by a sharp recovery (V-shape), the model mean-reverts too aggressively and fails to follow-through. Detect V-shapes (recent low within last ~10 days + strong rebound) and reduce mean-reversion strength; shift the forecast path upward and slightly steeper.",
    "Missed late-horizon breakout risk: the model rarely projects sustained moves into the upper tail (e.g., >68–70) even when realized does. If price crosses above recent 20–30d median by >~2 EUR and momentum is positive, cap/ceiling assumptions should be relaxed and an extra convex uplift added to days 15–30.",
    "Over-smoothing around spikes: the model produces a relatively flat path while realized outcomes trend strongly. When realized volatility in the last ~10 days is elevated (large daily ranges), increase forecast variance and allow trend continuation rather than damping; implement a trend-following adjustment proportional to recent slope."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model systematically underestimates the realized uptrend. Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger from ~day 10 onward).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: when the last 1–2 weeks show a rising regime (higher highs), the forecast responds too slowly. Add a momentum term based on the last 5–10 day slope to lift the whole path.",
    "Horizon-specific underpricing: errors are modest early but grow materially mid-to-late horizon (≈days 10–30). Use a horizon-weighted correction (e.g., linear ramp) rather than a flat adjustment.",
    "Peaks too low / rallies capped: during strong rallies the model produces local maxima ~2–4 EUR below realized. Increase forecast volatility/upside by scaling positive moves (asymmetric adjustment: boost positive deviations more than negative).",
    "Mean-reversion too strong: after dips, the model reverts to the prior mean, but the market continues trending higher. Reduce mean-reversion strength when recent realized volatility + positive drift are elevated."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent bearish bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 10–30).",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: when the market transitions from a dip to a sustained rally, the model follows too slowly and misses the slope. Add a momentum/trend-following term based on the last 5–10 days’ return, with stronger weight at longer horizons.",
    "Rally underestimation after local lows: following a multi-day decline into a trough, realized prices rebound more sharply than forecast. If recent 10-day drawdown exceeds a threshold, increase the next 2–3 weeks forecast path (convex rebound adjustment).",
    "Horizon-dependent error structure: short horizon (1–5d) errors are small and mixed; medium/long horizon (10–30d) errors become increasingly negative. Use a piecewise correction curve by forecast day rather than a single global bias.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are too flat versus realized swings during uptrends. Increase forecast variance conditional on positive momentum regimes (wider, higher path), but keep near-term adjustments modest."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias in rising markets: when the last 5–10 days show an upswing (positive slope), add an upward level shift to the whole path. In the examples, the model underpredicts by ~1–3 EUR early and ~4–7 EUR by weeks 3–4.",
